# Break the Shackles, Hero, Come Quickly!
Tamil title: விலங்கினை ஒடித்திட - வீரனே விரைந்து வா!

Summary of Arignar Anna's address at an anti-Hindi meeting in Thillai on 27 June 1960

Comrades,

We speak of the Tamil language, Tamil Nadu and Dravida Nadu because of our mother tongue. When we speak it, even a coward becomes a hero. We possess a language that inspires an uprising for liberation. Therefore, when it is endangered, everyone from the woman in the kitchen to the soldier in the army assumes a fighting posture.

If we have mouths, Tamil gives us speech. Without Tamil there is no speech, and what use is life as mute people? What use is life without rights? Language is essential to winning our rights. Everyone who loves Tamil and cares for the mother tongue must support the coming struggle to protect it, establish its rights and resist the imposition of Hindi.

When our friend K. R. Ramasamy said he was ready to lose his precious life in the anti-Hindi struggle, you applauded. I sat quietly wondering how many among you would shed blood or give their lives. I look upon you with pride.

No other party possesses support like this. You have gathered in your thousands, even sitting on rooftops and at the tops of branches without fearing they may break. What wondrous man have you come to see? I speak among you daily and live beside you. Why do thousands come to see the man they affectionately call Anna? I have not returned from America after mastering its political science. You come because Annadurai represents a great liberation movement and symbolises its ideal, and because you rejoice in the resolve to regain a lost country.

Our General Council has raised the battle flag against Hindi. At such a moment we must remember those who sacrificed themselves for our movement's aims.

A Tamil poet from a poor family in Tuticorin lived for the ideal. He came to our three-front agitation in 1953 on two legs; he now walks on one after offering the other to these rulers. Young people who see him tremble with anger; elders condemn the rulers; mothers wipe tears with their saris. We begin our struggle by remembering and saluting such people.

At Kallakudi, the railway station had been renamed Dalmiapuram as a symbol of northern economic domination. During the agitation to restore the name, a young man named Joseph came with two hands and lost one to Government gunfire. His mother had dreamed he would marry, have a child and embrace it with both arms. Seeing him now, she weeps over the rulers' cruelty.

In this district, Nellikuppam Majid was stabbed to death in daylight for no crime beyond campaigning against Congress. In Washermanpet, Madras, assailants entered comrade Pandian's house, threw chilli powder into his eyes and stabbed him to death. The life he lost gives living strength to this movement, and we salute him.

When we showed black flags to Nehru in January of the previous year, police encircled and drove the crowd. Nearly a lakh of people could move neither way and collided; two died trapped within it. I salute them too.

What shall you offer to the coming anti-Hindi struggle: hands, legs, eyes, or life itself? This meeting was called to ask what your contribution will be.

We have supplied every argument and statistic for our opposition. What answer came from State ministers or the northern leaders who control them? We said they disregarded us; they denied it while continuing to treat us as nobodies.

At this moment, when we ask how long injustice can be endured, President Rajendra Prasad has issued a new order: after 1965 English will be removed and Hindi must come. Whether you know it or not, you must accept it; the law has been made and acceptance is compulsory.

Many explained that Hindi should not become the common official language and lacked the necessary qualification, but the rulers would not listen. We held public meetings and conferences and sent resolutions of condemnation. They threw all of them into the dustbin and now impose Hindi with increasing speed.

People of the land and people of reason, what answer will you give? In 1965, Hindi is to enter Tamil Nadu like an invading spectre, knock on the closed door and open sleeping eyes. It says arrogantly: “I am only three hundred years old while Tamil is three thousand. I have neither grammar nor literature while Tamil has both. Nevertheless, I shall rule the Indian subcontinent.” I came to Thillai to ask how you will resist and destroy that arrogance.

Thillai houses Annamalai University, the temple of learning endowed by Annamalai. Its educated people need no explanation of Dravida Nadu, and this is not the hour for one. It is the hour to discover where the heroes who will oppose Hindi stand. Thillai has never answered “no” when asked. K. R. Ramasamy and I came to learn how many will join the struggle.

We have given the President until 30 August to withdraw the Hindi order. If no answer comes, we have decided to conduct protest meetings and processions and, if they are prohibited, to defy the prohibition.

When Nehru came to Madras, we decided to show black flags at Meenambakkam airport. To teach our members discipline, duty and nonviolence, we arranged a great meeting on the beach on 3 January. It had been advertised that Sampath, others and I would speak. Permission was initially granted; applications were made, notices issued, posters displayed, and thousands gathered.

At two in the afternoon, while I sat at headquarters, a police officer arrived. I thought he brought the expected order; instead he brought an order withdrawing permission and prohibiting the meeting.

What did the rulers imagine we would say? “Bring crowbars”? No. We would say: show Nehru black flags, remain disciplined, preserve peace, commit no violence, endure police blows, and if fired upon receive the bullets in your chests. Yet the Government banned the meeting. We decided to defy the ban and attend.

Sampath, Asaithambi, Dharmalingam, others and I left headquarters in an open car. Halfway, a senior police officer blocked us with his car, smiled, uttered an English arrest formula that the Tamil OCR has destroyed, arrested us and confined us in the Adyar police lock-up.

Officials strip clothing from black-marketeers, thieves, robbers and murderers lest they hang themselves after losing honour. Perhaps the rulers placed us in the same category: they took our upper garments and left us to sleep on the floor.

Police indiscriminately beat DMK members who peacefully showed black flags. In Saidapet jail I saw men who had left home in white garments now wearing what looked like black cloth. Their wounds had soaked the clothes red; after days without a change, the dried blood turned them dark. My heart trembled. Thinking, “It is because of me that you suffer this fate,” I shed tears without knowing it. One of those men was A. P. Arasu, later elected the DMK's first Mayor of Madras.

The people told the rulers, “You struck him with a baton; we give him authority to bear the Mayor's silver mace.” They taught the Government wisdom. Forgetting that lesson, Chief Minister Kamaraj recently said in Thanjavur that he possessed guns.

I respectfully tell the ruler: if you have the courage to shoot, shoot; we have the courage to receive the bullets in our chests. The day has come to answer those who boast of guns that we are ready for anything. I have no doubt that many from Thillai will come forward.

Hindi is introduced in a manner that will destroy Tamil: employment and promotion are made conditional on knowing it. If we give Hindi room here, we will become servants and our people will disappear from view. The source compares this prospect with Black Africans using period racial terminology; this is translated as historical rhetoric rather than a factual social comparison.

I was pleased to hear that at yesterday's meeting the Communists asked our cooperation on land reform. I ask them respectfully: whose side will you take on Hindi imposition? If you stand with northern domination on this issue, forgive me, but you will enter the list of those who betrayed this land.

Today our people possess an awareness of Tamils, Tamil Nadu and Tamil. Our race was ruled by the three crowned kings. Tamil monarchs conquered Burma, Kadaram and Kalinga, the speech says, invoking the *Kalingattuparani*. Knowing these accounts, we retain the thought that we once flourished and belong to a ruling lineage. Northern leaders want that memory erased.

Akbar did not reach this land; Aurangzeb could not; Chandragupta could not. They daydream of attaining a victory denied to Ashoka and Akbar. Tamils, how will you stop it?

If we fail and behave as hired hands, the world will mock us: “There is the Tamil, descended from people ruled by the three crowned kings, conquerors and maritime traders, now reduced to hired labour.” We struggle to protect our dignity.

Only yesterday the small country of Somaliland gained independence. Its people sang and danced in freedom, though the speech says it lacked mineral wealth. Our land has Tamil, grammar, literature and great epics. The crisis persists because we trusted, were deceived and betrayed.

In 1938, compulsory Hindi was introduced under Rajagopalachari. Because Periyar then had affection for me, I became commander of the anti-Hindi agitation. Three saffron-clad ascetics stood beside me: Eelathu Sivananda Adigal, Arunagiri Adigal and Sami Shanmugananda. Thousands of young people, elders and public-spirited mothers joined.

Seeing the resistance, Rajagopalachari later said in the Legislature, not merely at a public meeting, that had he known opposition would be so great he would not have imposed Hindi.

Our movement did not then enjoy today's support. In Chidambaram there were Manavazhagar, father of Anbazhagan; in Tiruchirappalli, Vedasalam and K. A. P. Viswanatham; in Salem, people such as Somasundaram; in Kanchipuram, Kalyanasundaram and C. V. M. Annamalai; in Madras, Dr Dharmambal. Only one or two in a hundred associated with us.

Today, as far as my eyes can see, countless young people, ploughing farmers, factory workers, labourers, elders and vigorous college students respond to the DMK by proclaiming, “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians!”

---NOTES---
The first-page scan resolves the title as “விலங்கினை ஒடித்திட - வீரனே விரைந்து வா!” and the date as 27 June 1960. The OCR's “27-56-1960” is not silently substituted in the preserved Tamil source.

Images 7–8 repeat material already present in Images 5–6 before continuing the speech. The bilingual file preserves that duplicated Tamil OCR verbatim, while the English translation presents the repeated passage once.

This is explicitly a published summary of the speech, not a verbatim transcript. Translation covers the complete recoverable summary.

The address contains militant sacrificial imagery but repeatedly instructs supporters to maintain discipline, peace and nonviolence. Both aspects are retained as historical political rhetoric.

Claims about injuries, deaths, ancient conquests, language ages, Somaliland, and statements by political leaders require independent historical verification before citation.

The source uses now-outdated racial terms in comparisons involving African Americans and Black Africans. The English translation uses current neutral descriptors and documents the historical usage without altering the Tamil source.
